        {
          "id": 1938682,
          "username": "Badhon_Rafi",
          "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/b/34f0e0/{size}.png",
          "created_at": "2026-11-12T13:53:30.008Z",
          "cooked": "<p>One thing I’d watch for with a one-row-per-product converter is variants. A lot of supplier sheets have several rows for the same product — one per size or colour — and if each row becomes its own draft product you end up with duplicates instead of one product with variants. The way I handle supplier files is to match on SKU first, keep all the variant rows grouped under their product, and then get a simple report back: how many SKUs matched, how many were updated, and which ones didn’t match anything so I can check them by hand. That report is the part that saves the most time on the next file, because the same mismatches tend to come back.</p>",
          "post_number": 2,
          "post_type": 1,
          "posts_count": 4,
